prompt:
Draft a professional, point-by-point rebuttal (response-to-reviewers) letter addressing every comment in the supplied document. Open with a brief thank-you to the editor and reviewers. Then, for each comment, in order and clearly numbered:

Work in this order:
1. restate the reviewer's concern verbatim or faithfully paraphrased;
2. give a respectful, substantive response that either agrees and describes the fix or politely justifies the original choice with evidence;
3. specify the exact change made and where (section, line, or figure), quoting revised text where helpful. Base every response only on the manuscript and provided materials; never invent new results or citations, and flag with [VERIFY] anything needing confirmation from the authors. Keep the tone courteous and confident, never defensive. If a reviewer comment is unclear or requires a decision only the authors can make, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise note the assumption. Save the letter as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize key responses in chat.

Write a structured abstract for the supplied manuscript, or from these details: {details}. Cover:

Work in this order:
1. Background/objective (1-2 sentences);
2. Methods (2-3 sentences);
3. Results (2-3 sentences with the key quantitative findings and statistics);
4. Conclusions (1-2 sentences stating significance and implications). Keep it within {word_limit} words. Use only information present in the source material; never fabricate results, numbers, or citations, and flag with [VERIFY] anything you cannot confirm. Ensure the abstract is self-contained, conveys the single key message, avoids undefined abbreviations, and matches the manuscript's claims without overstating them. If the key findings or word limit are missing or ambiguous in a way that changes the abstract, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumption. Save the abstract as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and paste the final text into chat with the word count.

Write a concise, professional cover letter (roughly one page) for submitting the supplied manuscript to {journal}, addressed to the Editor-in-Chief. Include:

Work in this order:
1. the manuscript title and a brief summary of the study;
2. the key findings and why they matter;
3. a specific, non-generic statement of why the journal and its readership are the right fit;
4. a statement of originality, that the work is not under concurrent consideration elsewhere, and that all authors approved the submission;
5. suggested (and, if relevant, opposed) reviewers. Draw all claims from the manuscript; never overstate findings or invent details, and flag with [VERIFY] anything you cannot confirm (e.g., author names, corresponding-author contact). If essential details are missing or ambiguous, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise note placeholders. Save the cover letter as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Based on the supplied protocol, data, or experimental notes, write a detailed Methods section suitable for a journal manuscript, written in past tense and the conventional impersonal scientific style. Cover, with clear subheadings:

Work in this order:
1. Study design and setting;
2. Materials, reagents, equipment, and where applicable catalog numbers, software, and versions;
3. Experimental procedures in enough operational detail for an independent lab to reproduce them;
4. Data collection methods and outcome measures;
5. Statistical analysis approach, including tests, assumptions, software, significance threshold, and handling of missing data. Use only details present in the supplied material; never invent parameters, reagents, or values, and flag any gap with [DETAIL NEEDED] rather than guessing. Note ethics/approval and reporting-standard requirements where relevant. If a missing detail would materially affect reproducibility, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise list the gaps. Save the Methods section as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Write a Results section from the supplied data, figures, and statistical outputs, reporting findings objectively in logical order with no interpretation, mechanism, or comparison to other work. For each result:

Work in this order:
1. state the finding clearly in plain scientific prose;
2. reference the relevant figure or table by number;
3. report the exact statistics present in the source (test statistic, degrees of freedom, p-value, confidence interval, and effect size). {focus} Use only values that appear in the supplied material; never fabricate or round away statistics or infer numbers that are not provided, and flag any missing or ambiguous value with [VERIFY] rather than guessing. Keep tense consistent and define abbreviations on first use. If a statistical output is unclear in a way that changes how a result is reported, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise note the gap. Save the Results section (with figure/table references) as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Write a Discussion section for the supplied manuscript or these results: {findings}. Structure it as:

Work in this order:
1. restate the main findings and their direct answer to the research question, without re-reporting every statistic;
2. interpret the findings in the context of existing literature, noting agreement and conflict with prior work;
3. discuss plausible mechanisms and the practical or theoretical implications;
4. candidly acknowledge limitations and their effect on interpretation;
5. suggest concrete future directions. Search the literature for real, verifiable sources to support every interpretive claim; never fabricate citations or overstate causation beyond what the data support, and flag with [VERIFY] anything you cannot confirm. If the findings provided are too sparse or ambiguous to interpret responsibly, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumptions. Save the Discussion section (with references) as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Write an Introduction section for a paper on: {topic}, using the classic funnel structure:

Work in this order:
1. broad context and significance of the field;
2. a focused review of key prior work, citing real, verifiable papers;
3. a clear statement of the gap or unanswered question;
4. the objective, hypothesis, and approach of the current study;
5. a brief signpost of what follows. Search the literature so that every citation is accurate (author, year, title); never fabricate references or attribute claims to the wrong source, and flag with [VERIFY] anything you cannot confirm. Build a logical argument that motivates the study rather than listing papers, and avoid overstating novelty. If the scope or intended contribution is ambiguous in a way that changes the framing, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumptions. Save the Introduction (with references) as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Prepare supplementary materials for the supplied manuscript, derived strictly from its content and data. Include, as relevant:

Work in this order:
1. detailed methods and parameters omitted from the main text for length;
2. supplementary figures, each with a self-contained caption;
3. supplementary tables with clear headers and units;
4. additional or sensitivity statistical analyses;
5. raw or summarized data tables. Number every item consistently (Fig. S1, Table S1, Supplementary Note 1, etc.), ensure each is referenced from the main text, and keep notation and units consistent with the main manuscript. Use only information present in the supplied material; never fabricate data, figures, or statistics, and flag any gap with [DATA NEEDED] rather than inventing content. If a needed input is missing or ambiguous, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise note the gap. Save the supplementary document (with tables and figure captions) as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize the items prepared in chat.

Audit the references in the supplied manuscript and produce {style}-formatted output.

Work in this order:
1. Verify each cited work actually exists and is correctly attributed (authors, year, title, venue), using web/literature search, and flag any reference you cannot confirm rather than assuming it is valid;
2. fix formatting inconsistencies and bring both in-text citations and the reference list into a consistent style;
3. identify text claims that need a citation but lack one;
4. suggest more recent, authoritative, or directly relevant references where appropriate, clearly marked as suggestions;
5. note duplicate, retracted, or broken entries. Never invent citations, DOIs, or bibliographic details; mark anything unverified as [VERIFY]. If the citation style is unspecified or the manuscript mixes styles in a way that changes the fix, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state the style you applied. Save a report (corrected reference list plus a list of issues and suggested citations) as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize the key findings in chat.

Analyze the supplied survey data (n={n} respondents). If the questionnaire structure, scale coding, or weighting variable is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Report response/completion rates and assess non-response and coverage bias against the target population;
2. Descriptive statistics for all items, flagging missingness and its mechanism;
3. Cross-tabulations with chi-square (report Cramer's V) for key comparisons, applying multiple-comparison correction;
4. Likert analysis: means, medians, distributions, treating ordinal scales appropriately;
5. Factor analysis or PCA for scale items, reporting reliability (Cronbach's alpha);
6. Regression models predicting {outcome} with effect sizes and 95% CIs, applying survey weights if provided. Use careful causal language. Set a random seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note sampling limitations.

Perform text mining on the supplied corpus ({corpus_type}). If language, time field, or unit of analysis is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Preprocess: tokenize, normalize case, remove stopwords, lemmatize, and document filtering decisions;
2. Generate word-frequency and TF-IDF analysis;
3. Run LDA topic modeling with {n_topics} topics (default 10), reporting coherence to justify topic count;
4. Perform named entity recognition;
5. Analyze sentiment distribution with a validated model, noting its limits;
6. Visualize word clouds, topic networks, and temporal trends. Be careful that frequencies reflect the corpus, not a representative population; avoid overgeneralizing. Set a random seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, topic/term tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key themes in chat, name the files, and note corpus and model limitations.

Perform a demographic analysis for: {population}. If the geographic level, age bands, or data vintage is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Compile population data (age, sex, ethnicity, education, income) from real, cited sources (e.g., census/UN/national statistics), recording vintage and definitions;
2. Create population pyramids and distribution charts;
3. Analyze trends over {period} with appropriate rate standardization;
4. Compute dependency ratios and aging indicators;
5. Compare with national/regional benchmarks;
6. Project future demographics using the cohort-component method, stating fertility/mortality/migration assumptions and showing uncertainty bands across scenarios. Do not fabricate figures; cite each source. Record package versions and set seeds for any stochastic projections. Save figures as PNG, projection tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note data and assumption limitations.

Analyze the supplied social network data. If directedness, edge weighting, or node identity is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Compute network statistics (size, density, diameter, average path length, clustering coefficient), specifying directed vs undirected;
2. Calculate centrality measures (degree, betweenness, closeness, eigenvector);
3. Detect communities (Louvain), reporting modularity and stability across runs;
4. Identify bridges and brokers (e.g., high betweenness, structural holes);
5. Analyze information-flow patterns;
6. Visualize with community coloring and centrality-based node sizing. Compare key metrics against a null model (configuration/random graph) to gauge significance. Set a random seed for stochastic algorithms and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, node/edge metric tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key findings in chat, name the files, and note boundary-specification and sampling limitations.

Analyze sentiment in the supplied social media data about: {topic}. If platform, language, or the date field is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Classify posts as positive/negative/neutral using a validated NLP model, reporting accuracy/F1 against any labeled subset;
2. Track sentiment over time with confidence bands;
3. Identify key themes driving positive and negative sentiment;
4. Break down sentiment by demographic or geographic group if available, with effect sizes and CIs;
5. Detect bot or inauthentic activity and assess its impact;
6. Generate a sentiment report with actionable, caveated insights. Stress that the sample is not a representative population; avoid causal claims. Set a random seed and record package/model versions. Save figures as PNG, classified-data and summary tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note sampling and model limitations.

Evaluate the causal impact of {policy} on {outcome} using {method}. If treatment timing, the comparison group, or the running/instrument variable is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions; cite real data sources and do not fabricate.

Work in this order:
1. Define treatment and control groups and the identifying assumptions for the chosen method;
2. Apply it with appropriate, clustered standard errors;
3. Test the key identifying assumption (parallel trends for DiD, continuity/bandwidth for RDD, balance for PSM, instrument relevance/exclusion for IV);
4. Estimate the treatment effect with 95% confidence intervals and effect size;
5. Run robustness checks (placebo/falsification tests, alternative specifications, sensitivity to bandwidth/matching);
6. Interpret findings with careful causal language and discuss policy implications. Set a seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, estimate tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note threats to validity.

Analyze education outcomes from the supplied data. If outcome definition, grouping, or available covariates are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Describe student demographics and performance distributions, flagging missingness;
2. Compare results between {groups} with effect sizes, 95% CIs, and multiple-comparison correction;
3. Analyze achievement gaps by subgroup;
4. Build a predictive model for {outcome} with proper train/test splits and cross-validation, reporting calibrated performance and key predictors while distinguishing association from causation;
5. Evaluate the effect of {intervention} if intervention data are available, controlling for SES and prior achievement and accounting for clustering within schools/classes;
6. Summarize practical implications. Set a random seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, result tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note confounding and generalizability limitations.

Analyze public opinion on {topic} from the supplied polling data. If the weighting targets, question wording, or survey mode are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions; cite real comparison polls and do not fabricate figures.

Work in this order:
1. Weight responses to known population margins for representativeness, documenting the scheme;
2. Calculate point estimates with design-based margins of error;
3. Analyze trends over time, distinguishing real change from sampling noise;
4. Break down by demographics (age, gender, education, party, region) with CIs and multiple-comparison correction;
5. Identify opinion drivers via weighted regression, using careful (non-causal) language;
6. Compare with historical polls and flag significant shifts versus house/mode effects. Set a seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, estimate tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note coverage and nonresponse limitations.

Analyze migration patterns for {region}. If the spatial unit, time window, or flow definition is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions; cite real public sources (e.g., UN DESA, national statistics) and do not fabricate.

Work in this order:
1. Map origin-destination flows from the supplied or public data, noting definitions and coverage;
2. Quantify net migration rates per 1,000 with consistent denominators;
3. Analyze push and pull factors;
4. Model determinants with a gravity model (log-linear/Poisson PPML), reporting coefficients with 95% CIs and goodness of fit;
5. Assess demographic impact on sending and receiving regions;
6. Visualize flows on maps and chord diagrams. Use careful causal language and check robustness to specification. Set a seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, flow and model tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note data and measurement limitations.

Measure inequality in {dimension} using the supplied data. If the income/wealth concept, equivalence scale, or weighting is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions; cite real benchmarks and do not fabricate.

Work in this order:
1. Calculate the Gini coefficient (with bootstrap CIs) and plot the Lorenz curve, applying sample weights;
2. Compute percentile ratios (90/10, 80/20) and top/bottom shares;
3. Decompose inequality by {subgroups} (within vs between);
4. Analyze trends over time, adjusting for inflation/currency where relevant;
5. Compare with international benchmarks;
6. Identify key drivers using decomposition methods (Blinder-Oaxaca, Theil), reporting contributions with uncertainty. Set a seed for bootstrap/resampling and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, index and decomposition tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note definition-sensitivity and top-coding limitations.

Analyze data from the supplied behavioral experiment ({design}). If the dependent variable, condition coding, or repeated-measures structure is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Check randomization balance across conditions;
2. Analyze manipulation checks;
3. Test main hypotheses with {analysis_type}, verifying its assumptions (normality, sphericity, homogeneity) and using robust alternatives if violated;
4. Analyze moderators and mediators with appropriate models;
5. Report effect sizes (Cohen's d, partial eta-squared) with 95% CIs and correct for multiple comparisons;
6. Check for demand, order, and attrition effects;
7. Produce APA-formatted results with publication-quality figures. Do not report observed post-hoc power; report minimum detectable effects or a sensitivity analysis at the achieved sample size, and use careful causal language. Set a random seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, result tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note limitations.

Perform content analysis on the supplied documents/media. If the unit of analysis, category definitions, or language is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Develop an explicit codebook for {categories} with definitions and decision rules;
2. Apply automated coding via NLP and validate against a hand-coded subset;
3. Calculate inter-rater reliability (Cohen's kappa, or Krippendorff's alpha for multiple coders) and report agreement;
4. Analyze code frequency and co-occurrence with effect sizes;
5. Compare content across {groups} or time periods using appropriate tests with multiple-comparison correction;
6. Identify dominant frames and narratives, distinguishing prevalence from interpretation. Set a random seed and record package/model versions. Save figures as PNG, coded-data and frequency tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key findings in chat, name the files, and note coding-validity and corpus limitations.

Set up and run a CFD simulation for: {geometry}. If geometry dimensions, fluid properties, or flow regime are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Define the geometry, characteristic length, and meshing strategy, computing the Reynolds number to justify the regime;
2. Set boundary conditions: {conditions}, using consistent SI units;
3. State the governing equations (continuity, Navier-Stokes), assumptions (incompressible/steady, etc.), and select a turbulence model (k-epsilon, k-omega SST, or LES) with rationale and y+ targets;
4. Run to convergence, monitoring scaled residuals and key quantities;
5. Verify mesh-independence via grid refinement (report GCI/discretization error) and check numerical stability (CFL);
6. Validate against an analytic case or published benchmark, reporting error. Post-process velocity/pressure fields and drag/lift coefficients, with streamlines and contours. Record solver settings and package versions. Save figures as PNG, coefficient/convergence tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize results in chat, name the files, and note modeling limitations.

Before expensive execution, inspect available resources on the BYOK host and required binaries. If the full workload exceeds host capacity, use configured Modal compute only when authorized; otherwise run a bounded pilot or produce a validated execution plan. Never imply that an unexecuted full run completed.

Process the supplied signal data. If sample rate, channel layout, or units are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Characterize the signal (sample rate, duration, channels, units) and verify the Nyquist criterion;
2. Apply filtering: {filter_spec}, reporting filter type/order, using zero-phase filtering where phase matters, and showing the frequency response;
3. Compute FFT and power spectral density with appropriate windowing;
4. Perform time-frequency analysis (STFT, wavelet transform);
5. Extract features: {features};
6. Detect events or anomalies with stated thresholds;
7. Compare before/after processing and quantify SNR improvement and any distortion. Set a random seed where stochastic and record package versions. Save figures (spectra, spectrograms) as PNG, feature tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note artifact and resolution limitations.

Design a control system for: {plant}. Requirements: {specs}. If plant parameters, actuator limits, or sample time are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Model the plant as a transfer function or state-space, listing assumptions and units;
2. Analyze open-loop poles/zeros, stability, and performance;
3. Design a {controller_type} controller;
4. Tune parameters to meet the requirements (rise time, overshoot, steady-state error);
5. Analyze the closed loop via step response, Bode plot, and root locus, reporting gain and phase margins;
6. Verify robustness to model uncertainty, disturbances, and actuator saturation, and confirm the numerical integration step size yields a stable, converged simulation. Record package versions. Save figures (step, Bode, root locus) as PNG, performance-metric tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize results in chat, name the files, and note model-fidelity limitations.

Analyze heat transfer in: {system}. If geometry, material properties, or boundary temperatures/fluxes are ambiguous and would materially change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Define geometry, materials, and thermal boundary conditions with SI units;
2. Set up the governing heat equation with relevant modes (conduction k, convection h, radiation), computing Biot/Nusselt/Rayleigh numbers to justify correlations;
3. Solve for the temperature distribution analytically or via FEM/finite-difference, stating steady vs transient;
4. Calculate heat flux and total heat-transfer rate;
5. Analyze the thermal-resistance network;
6. For numerical solutions, verify mesh/step-size convergence and stability and validate against an analytic limiting case, reporting error. Visualize temperature contours and heat-flow paths. Record package versions and any seeds. Save figures as PNG, result tables (temperatures, fluxes, resistances) as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize results in chat, name the files, and note assumption limitations.

Analyze vibrations in the supplied data or system: {system}. If sample rate, units (g vs m/s2), or operating speed are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Compute the frequency spectrum (FFT) with appropriate windowing, verifying Nyquist;
2. Identify natural frequencies and mode shapes;
3. Estimate damping ratios (half-power bandwidth or log-decrement);
4. Perform order tracking for rotating machinery, referencing running speed;
5. Compare RMS/peak levels against operational limits and standards ({standard}), stating units;
6. Identify fault signatures (imbalance, misalignment, bearing, gear) if diagnostic. Generate waterfall and Campbell diagrams. For any modal model, validate against analytic single-DOF cases and report error; record package versions and seeds. Save figures as PNG, frequency/feature tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note resolution and measurement limitations.

Simulate the process: {process}. If feed compositions, kinetics/rate data, or operating ranges are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Define the process flow diagram and unit operations;
2. Specify feed compositions and operating conditions with consistent units;
3. Simulate using discrete-event (SimPy) or continuous methods as appropriate, stating governing balances and assumptions;
4. Close mass and energy balances and verify residuals are within tolerance;
5. Optimize for {objective} (yield, cost, energy, or throughput), reporting the optimum and constraints;
6. Perform sensitivity analysis on key parameters and, for stochastic models, run replications with confidence intervals. Verify numerical convergence/step-size independence. Set a random seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, balance and result tables (yields, throughputs, utilities) as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize results in chat, name the files, and note modeling limitations.

Perform reliability analysis for: {system}. If the failure-data structure, censoring, or operating context are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Fit failure-time distributions (Weibull, exponential, lognormal), handling censored data and comparing fits via AIC and probability plots;
2. Estimate MTTF/MTBF and failure rates with confidence intervals;
3. Construct a reliability block diagram or fault tree;
4. Calculate system-level reliability R(t) and availability;
5. Perform failure-mode analysis (FMEA) with risk priority numbers;
6. Recommend optimal preventive-maintenance intervals, justifying the cost/risk trade-off. State all time units and assumed distributions. Set a seed for any bootstrap/Monte Carlo and record package versions. Save figures (reliability curves, hazard plots) as PNG, parameter and FMEA tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note data-quantity and distributional limitations.

Analyze the supplied sensor/IoT data ({sensors}). If sampling rates, units, or the timestamp/timezone convention are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Clean and synchronize multi-sensor streams to a common time base, documenting resampling;
2. Detect and handle missing data and outliers, recording the method and counts;
3. Extract statistical and frequency-domain features per sensor;
4. Correlate sensor readings with {events}, distinguishing correlation from causation;
5. Build anomaly-detection models for predictive maintenance with proper train/test splits and cross-validation, reporting precision/recall and false-alarm rate;
6. Create a monitoring dashboard visualization. Set a random seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, feature and anomaly-event tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note sensor-drift, labeling, and generalizability limitations.

Plan a path for a {robot_type} robot in the supplied environment. If map units/resolution, start/goal, or robot footprint and kinematic limits are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Define the configuration space and inflate obstacles by the robot footprint;
2. Apply {algorithm} planning (RRT*, A*, PRM, or potential field);
3. Optimize for {objective} (shortest, smoothest, fastest, or energy-optimal);
4. Smooth the path for kinematic/dynamic feasibility (curvature, velocity limits);
