6. Visualize the planned path in 2D/3D with obstacles. For sampling-based planners, run multiple seeds and report path length, clearance, and computation-time statistics with variability. Record package versions. Save figures as PNG, path and metric tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize results in chat, name the files, and note completeness/optimality limitations.

Perform structural analysis on: {structure}. Loads: {loads}. If member geometry, material properties, or support conditions are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Define the structural model (beams, frames, plates) and coordinate system;
2. Apply material properties and boundary conditions with consistent SI units, stating the governing equations (equilibrium, beam/plate theory);
3. Solve for reactions, internal forces, and displacements;
4. Check stresses against material limits using a von Mises or relevant criterion and verify the achieved factor against the required safety factor = {sf};
