6. Visualize the deformed shape and stress distribution. Validate against an analytic case (e.g., cantilever/simply-supported beam) and report error; if meshed, confirm convergence. Record package versions. Save figures as PNG, reaction/stress tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize results in chat, name the files, and note assumption limitations.

Perform finite element analysis for: {problem}. If geometry, material properties, or units are ambiguous and would materially change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Define the geometry and create the mesh, noting element type and order;
2. Assign material properties in consistent SI units;
3. Apply boundary conditions and loads: {conditions}, stating the governing equations and assumptions;
4. Solve the FEM problem, confirming solver convergence;
5. Post-process the relevant fields (stress/strain, displacement, temperature, or potential);
6. Verify mesh convergence via systematic refinement, reporting the discretization error and a converged quantity of interest;
7. Validate against an analytic or benchmark solution, reporting error, and visualize results with contour plots. Record package versions and mesh statistics. Save figures as PNG, result and convergence tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize results in chat, name the files, and note modeling limitations.

Before expensive execution, inspect available resources on the BYOK host and required binaries. If the full workload exceeds host capacity, use configured Modal compute only when authorized; otherwise run a bounded pilot or produce a validated execution plan. Never imply that an unexecuted full run completed.

